---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ConceptPageHero from '../../components/concepts/ConceptPageHero.astro';
import ConceptPager from '../../components/concepts/ConceptPager.astro';
import { getConcept } from '../../lib/concepts';

const c = getConcept('rotation');
---

<Layout title={`${c.title} | Identity Engineering`}>
	<main class="min-h-screen">
		<ConceptPageHero
			scene={c.scene}
			tag={`${c.tag} · ${c.layerLabel}`}
			title={c.title}
			lede={c.blurb}
			scrollHint={c.scrollHint}
		/>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-8 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/55">
				<div class="mb-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Definition</div>
				<h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white md:text-4xl">
					Stability through spin, and the tension it creates.
				</h2>
				<p>
					<strong class="text-white/80">Rotation</strong> is the gyroscopic stabilization of the Identity Stem.
					Like a spinning top or Earth on its axis: angular momentum keeps the stem upright.
					High coherent spin means low wobble. Slow, incoherent motion means precession and collapse under small torques.
				</p>
				<p>
					The framework includes a <strong class="text-white/80">tangential stabilizing force</strong>:
					habits and stem-coherent Owned Acts that act optimally relative to the axis.
					They maximize stability and structured substance growth without destabilizing the stem.
				</p>
				<p>
					However, Rotation has a double relationship to tension: it both <strong class="text-white/80">stabilizes existing tension</strong> and <strong class="text-white/80">generates new tension</strong>.
				</p>
				<div class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-black/30 p-6">
					<div class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[3px] text-white/40 mb-3">Working variables</div>
					<p class="text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
						You can model rotation quality with three practical variables:
						<code class="text-violet-300">ω (coherent spin), τ (external torque), W (wobble under load)</code>.
						Goal: raise coherent spin and reduce wobble without suppressing adaptation.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02] px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
				<div class="mb-6 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Mechanics</div>
				<ul class="space-y-6 text-lg text-white/55">
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Axis:</strong> the Identity Stem (Time layer). Rotation does not invent the axis; it stabilizes it.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Spin:</strong> coherent, repeated motion around that axis (practices, rhythms, ritualized commitments that reinforce stem coherence).
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Tangential force:</strong> habits that feed substance into the system without lateral thrashing.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Wobble:</strong> quality metric for how much the stem precesses under load.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
				<div class="mb-6 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Rotation and Tension</div>
				<p class="text-lg text-white/55 mb-6">
					Rotation interacts with inner tension in two opposing ways:
				</p>
				<ul class="space-y-5 text-lg text-white/55">
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Stabilizing effect:</strong> Strong, coherent rotation increases resistance to disturbances (gyroscopic effect). This allows an identity to hold significantly more internal tension (conflicting demands, emotional load, external pressure) without losing its fundamental orientation or coherence.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Tension-generating effect:</strong> Rotation simultaneously produces its own centrifugal tension: a drive toward expansion, differentiation, and in extreme cases fragmentation. This self-generated tension acts like a centrifugal force: it pushes parts of the identity outward and creates new internal stress that must be absorbed.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p class="mt-6 text-lg text-white/55">
					For rotation to remain stabilizing rather than fragmenting, sufficient <strong class="text-white/80">Mass</strong> and internal cohesion are required. Without enough Mass, high rotational speed shifts from protective to destabilizing.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02] px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/55">
				<p>
					<strong class="text-white/80">Engineering work:</strong> Increase productive spin and reduce thrash.
					Not every motion is good. Only rotation that is coherent with State Differential and Vision Gradient
					builds gyroscopic stability worth having. Rotation is not an end in itself. It is a tool to make higher levels of productive tension sustainable.
				</p>
				<div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-black/30 p-6">
					<div class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[3px] text-white/40 mb-3">Practice loop</div>
					<ul class="space-y-3 text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
						<li>Identify one recurring action that increases stem coherence.</li>
						<li>Remove one recurring action that adds lateral thrash.</li>
						<li>Track wobble after high-pressure events for two weeks.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-cyan-400/20 bg-cyan-400/[0.04] p-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-white/60">
					<div class="mb-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-[3px] text-cyan-300/80">Foundations anchor</div>
					<p>
						The conserved character of angular momentum (and therefore of coherent spin around an axis) is a direct consequence of rotational symmetry, as proven by Noether’s theorem.
						See <a href="/foundations/noether-symmetry-and-conservation" class="text-white/80 underline decoration-white/25 underline-offset-4 transition hover:text-white hover:decoration-white/50">Emmy Noether: Symmetry and Conservation Laws</a>.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<ConceptPager id="rotation" />
	</main>
</Layout>
